    en: `
<strong>Government and Political System</strong>

Lithuania is an independent <strong>parliamentary republic</strong> located on the eastern coast of the Baltic Sea. Together with Latvia and Estonia, it forms the three Baltic States. The modern Republic of Lithuania restored its independence in <strong>1990</strong>, becoming the first Soviet republic to officially declare its withdrawal from the Soviet Union.

The country's supreme legal document is the <strong>Constitution of the Republic of Lithuania</strong>, adopted by national referendum in <strong>1992</strong>. It establishes the principles of democracy, separation of powers, the rights of citizens and the structure of government.

The <strong>President of Lithuania</strong> is the head of state and is elected by popular vote for a <strong>five-year term</strong>. The president represents the country internationally, appoints the prime minister with parliamentary approval, serves as commander-in-chief of the armed forces and plays an important role in shaping Lithuania's foreign policy.

<strong>Executive Power</strong>

Executive authority is exercised by the <strong>Government of Lithuania</strong>, headed by the <strong>Prime Minister</strong>. The government is responsible for the country's economy, education, healthcare, transport, social policy, implementation of laws and preparation of the national budget.

<strong>Legislative Power</strong>

Legislative authority belongs to the <strong>unicameral Parliament — the Seimas of the Republic of Lithuania</strong>. It consists of <strong>141 members</strong>, elected for a term of <strong>four years</strong>. The Seimas adopts laws, approves the state budget, supervises the government and ratifies international treaties.

<strong>History of Independence</strong>

Lithuania has one of the richest historical traditions in Europe. During the thirteenth century it became the center of the <strong>Grand Duchy of Lithuania</strong>, one of the largest and most powerful states in Europe, stretching from the Baltic Sea to the Black Sea. In <strong>1253</strong>, <strong>Mindaugas</strong> was crowned as the only King of Lithuania.

Later Lithuania entered a union with Poland and, in <strong>1569</strong>, became part of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth. Following the partitions of Poland at the end of the eighteenth century, Lithuanian territory became part of the Russian Empire.

Modern Lithuania first regained its independence in <strong>1918</strong>. In <strong>1940</strong>, the country was incorporated into the Soviet Union. One of the most significant moments in modern Lithuanian history was the <strong>Baltic Way</strong> in <strong>1989</strong>, when nearly two million people from Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia formed a human chain stretching more than 600 kilometres.

On <strong>11 March 1990</strong>, Lithuania became the first Soviet republic to declare the restoration of its independence. Following the events of August <strong>1991</strong>, its independence was internationally recognized.

<strong>Modern Lithuania</strong>

In <strong>2004</strong>, Lithuania joined both the <strong>European Union</strong> and <strong>NATO</strong>, strengthening its political, economic and security ties with Europe. Since <strong>2015</strong>, the country's official currency has been the <strong>euro</strong>. Lithuania is also a member of the <strong>Schengen Area</strong>, the United Nations, the Council of Europe, the World Trade Organization and the OECD.

Today Lithuania is divided into <strong>60 municipalities</strong>. The capital, <strong>Vilnius</strong>, is the country's largest city and serves as its political, cultural, educational and economic center.

Although relatively small in size, Lithuania is considered one of the most dynamic countries in the Baltic region. It has developed a modern economy, advanced technology, efficient transport infrastructure and strong international partnerships while carefully preserving the rich historical heritage of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.
`,
