        en: `
<strong>The Palace of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania</strong> is one of Lithuania's most important historical landmarks. Located beside Vilnius Cathedral at the foot of Castle Hill, it served for centuries as the political residence of the rulers of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, one of Europe's largest medieval states.

The first stone buildings appeared here during the <strong>fifteenth century</strong> under <strong>Vytautas the Great</strong>. Later the Jagiellonian dynasty expanded the palace into an impressive Renaissance residence comparable to the finest royal courts of Europe.

Foreign ambassadors were welcomed here, international treaties were negotiated, state councils were held and magnificent ceremonies took place within its halls. The palace became the political heart of the Grand Duchy and strengthened Vilnius as one of Eastern Europe's leading capitals.

After the wars of the seventeenth century the palace suffered severe damage, and in the early nineteenth century its remaining structures were almost completely demolished under Russian rule. For more than two centuries only archaeological remains marked the site.

Following extensive archaeological excavations, the palace was carefully reconstructed in the early twenty-first century. Today it houses the <strong>National Museum</strong>, displaying archaeological discoveries, historical weapons, works of art, reconstructed royal interiors and exhibitions dedicated to the history of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.

Today the Palace of the Grand Dukes stands as one of Lithuania's strongest symbols of national identity, allowing visitors to explore the place where many of the country's most important political decisions were once made.
`,
